Now I have everything needed. Let me compile the full report.

## 1. `frontend/src/data/` directory

Only one file exists: `frontend\src\data\paymentPreviewFallbacks.js` (15 lines total).

Full content:
```js
const paymentPreviewFallbacks = {
  invalidReferences: new Set(["S12346B"]),
  employees: [
    { employeeReference: "S12345A", employeeName: "John Tan", department: "Training", grossPay: 850, cpfAmount: 170, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 677, bankCode: "OCBC", bankAccountNumber: "•••• 1234", fallbackStatus: "ready" },
    { employeeReference: "S12346B", employeeName: "Sarah Lim", department: "Ambulance", grossPay: 920, cpfAmount: 184, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 733, bankCode: "DBS", bankAccountNumber: "•••• 5678", fallbackStatus: "invalid", issueReason: "Invalid bank account number format." },
    { employeeReference: "S12347C", employeeName: "David Ng", department: "Training", grossPay: 710, cpfAmount: 142, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 565, bankCode: "", bankAccountNumber: "", fallbackStatus: "missing", missingFields: ["bankCode", "bankAccountNumber"] },
    { employeeReference: "S12348D", employeeName: "Rachel Tan", department: "Medical", grossPay: 1050, cpfAmount: 210, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 837, bankCode: "", bankAccountNumber: "", fallbackStatus: "missing", missingFields: ["bankAccountNumber"] },
    { employeeReference: "S12349E", employeeName: "Marcus Chen", department: "Training", grossPay: 680, cpfAmount: 136, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 541, bankCode: "UOB", bankAccountNumber: "•••• 9012", fallbackStatus: "ready" },
    { employeeReference: "S12350F", employeeName: "Yvonne Lau", department: "Admin", grossPay: 760, cpfAmount: 152, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 605, bankCode: "OCBC", bankAccountNumber: "•••• 3456", fallbackStatus: "ready" },
    { employeeReference: "S12351G", employeeName: "Kevin Wong", department: "Ambulance", grossPay: 890, cpfAmount: 178, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 709, bankCode: "DBS", bankAccountNumber: "•••• 7880", fallbackStatus: "ready" },
    { employeeReference: "S12352H", employeeName: "Nur Hidayah", department: "Training", grossPay: 810, cpfAmount: 162, sdlAmount: 3, approvedNetPay: 645, bankCode: "UOB", bankAccountNumber: "•••• 2345", fallbackStatus: "ready" },
  ],
};

export default paymentPreviewFallbacks;
```

Note: `invalidReferences` is exported but never consumed anywhere else in the codebase (only `.employees` is used).

## 2. Grep results for "paymentPreviewFallbacks", "Fallback", "mock"

**"paymentPreviewFallbacks"** — only two hits, both live/reachable code:
- `frontend/src/data/paymentPreviewFallbacks.js:1,15` — definition/export.
- `frontend/src/utils/paymentPreviewData.js:2,46` — the sole importer.

**"Fallback"/"fallback"** (case-insensitive) — relevant hits beyond generic axios-error fallbacks in `frontend/src/utils.js` (`saveBlobResponse`, `getErrorMessage` — unrelated, just default-value parameter naming):
- `frontend/src/utils/paymentPreviewData.js` lines 1, 2, 13, 27, 43, 46, 48, 51 — this is the real consumer file (detailed in section on that file below).
- `frontend/src/pages/PaymentPreviewPage.jsx` lines 280, 313 — uses `employee.fallbackOnly` to `disabled` the "Add/Update details" and "Update Employee" buttons when a row came from demo/fallback data (so users can't try to edit demo records).
- `frontend/src/pages/ReviewEmployeesPage.jsx` line 69 — sets `fallbackOnly: false` after a real bank-details update succeeds (this file consumes rows built by `paymentPreviewData.js`/passed via route state from `PaymentPreviewPage`, it does not import the fallback data file itself).

**"mock"** (case-insensitive) — no matches anywhere in `frontend/src`.

Conclusion: this is **live, reachable fallback/demo logic**, gated by an explicit feature flag (`VITE_ENABLE_DEMO_DATA`), not dead code. See `frontend/src/config/demoMode.js`:
```js
export const demoModeEnabled = import.meta.env.VITE_ENABLE_DEMO_DATA === "true";
```
It is used in exactly two places in `paymentPreviewData.js`:
- `buildPaymentPreviewRows()` — falls back to `demoRows()` only when there's no real preview and no missingDetails, and only if `demoModeEnabled`.
- `getDemoPaymentPreview()` — returns a fake "success" preview object when the real API call throws an unrecognized error and demo mode is on; called from `PaymentPreviewPage.jsx`'s `checkReadiness()` catch block (line ~110) as a last-resort UI fallback so the page doesn't just show a raw error when in demo mode.

## 3. `frontend/src/pages/PaymentPreviewPage.jsx` (329 lines, full content shown above)

Key integration points:
- Imports `getEligiblePeriods`, `getPaymentPreview` from `../api/paymentApi`.
- Imports `buildPaymentPreviewRows, getDemoPaymentPreview, getPreviewCounts, matchesEmployeeSearch` from `../utils/paymentPreviewData`.
- `checkReadiness()`: calls `getPaymentPreview`; on error, checks error code (`MISSING_BANK_DETAILS`, `PAYROLL_LINES_MISSING`); for any other error it calls `getDemoPaymentPreview()` and uses it if demo mode is enabled, otherwise sets a real error message.
- Displays `<Alert>Demo data is enabled...</Alert>` when `preview?.demo` is true (line 190).
- Row-level `fallbackOnly` flag disables the bank-update buttons (lines 280, 313).

## 4. `frontend/src/api/paymentApi.js` (full content, 13 lines)

```js
import apiClient from "./client";

export const getEligiblePeriods = async () => (await apiClient.get("/payments/eligible-periods")).data;
export const getPaymentPreview = async (payPeriodId) => (await apiClient.get("/payments/preview", { params: { payPeriodId } })).data;
export const generatePayment = async (payPeriodId) => (await apiClient.post("/payments/generate", { payPeriodId })).data;
export const getPaymentStatistics = async () => (await apiClient.get("/payments/dashboard/statistics")).data;
export const getPaymentBatches = async (params = {}) => (await apiClient.get("/payments", { params })).data;
export const getPaymentBatch = async (batchId) => (await apiClient.get(`/payments/${batchId}`)).data;
export const downloadPaymentFile = (batchId) => apiClient.get(`/payments/${batchId}/file`, { responseType: "blob" });
export const retryHrms = async (batchId) => (await apiClient.post(`/payments/${batchId}/retry-hrms`)).data;
export const cancelPaymentBatch = async (batchId, reason) => (await apiClient.patch(`/payments/${batchId}/cancel`, { reason })).data;
export const getBatchPayslips = async (batchId) => (await apiClient.get(`/payments/${batchId}/payslips`)).data;
```
No mock/fallback logic here — it's a pure API client; all fallback behavior lives in `paymentPreviewData.js`.

## 5. `backend/src/db/seeders/` listing and content

Files:
- `20260720000200-authentication-users.js` (63 lines) — seeds 3 users: manager (`manager@payroll.local`/`Manager123!`), employee (`employee@payroll.local`/`Employee123!`, linked to `staff_id: "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111"` — note this staff_id doesn't appear to be created by any seeder, meaning that FK reference is dangling unless created elsewhere), and a disabled demo user. Passwords come from env vars with dev fallbacks. Uses raw SQL `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`.
- `20260726000100-development-payment-test-data.js` (433 lines) — full content already read above. This is the payment-related dev seeder. Key facts:
  - Guards against production via `assertDevelopment()`.
  - Generates deterministic UUIDs via prefix helpers (`staffId`, `periodId`, `payrollLineId`, `batchId`, `batchItemId`, `payslipId`, `approvalId`).
  - 8 staff members, 8 pay-period "scenarios" (READY, MISSING-BANK, HRMS-FAILED, COMPLETED, CANCELLED, PENDING-APPROVAL, EMPTY, GENERATED) covering all payment-preview/generation edge cases.
  - Populates `staff`, `pay_period`, `approval`, `payroll_line`, `payment_batch`, `payment_batch_item`, `payslip` tables via `upsertRows` (bulkInsert with `updateOnDuplicate`/`upsertKeys: ["id"]`).
  - `down()` calls `removeOwnedData()` which deletes by matching `batch_reference LIKE 'DEV-PAY-%'` or a hardcoded legacy ID range (`LEGACY_STAFF_COUNT = 102`, `LEGACY_PERIOD_COUNT = 49` — suggesting this seeder previously seeded more rows and was pared down, leaving cleanup logic for old-run leftovers).
  - Depends on the authentication-users seeder having already run (checks `MANAGER_ID` exists in `user_account`, throws if not).
  - Runnable standalone via `npm run db:seed:payments:test` (see package.json script below).

No other seeders exist relevant to Staff/PayPeriod/PayrollLine/Approval/User — these two are the entirety of `backend/src/db/seeders/`.

## 6. `backend/src/models/index.js` associations (full file, 56 lines)

Relevant to the payment domain:
```js
db.PayPeriod.hasMany(db.PayrollLine, { foreignKey: "pay_period_id", as: "payrollLines" });
db.PayrollLine.belongsTo(db.PayPeriod, { foreignKey: "pay_period_id", as: "payPeriod" });
db.Staff.hasMany(db.PayrollLine, { foreignKey: "staff_id", as: "payrollLines" });
db.PayrollLine.belongsTo(db.Staff, { foreignKey: "staff_id", as: "staff" });

db.PayPeriod.hasMany(db.Approval, { foreignKey: "pay_period_id", as: "approvals" });
db.Approval.belongsTo(db.PayPeriod, { foreignKey: "pay_period_id", as: "payPeriod" });

db.PayPeriod.hasMany(db.PaymentBatch, { foreignKey: "pay_period_id", as: "paymentBatches" });
db.PaymentBatch.belongsTo(db.PayPeriod, { foreignKey: "pay_period_id", as: "payPeriod" });
db.User.hasMany(db.PaymentBatch, { foreignKey: "generated_by", as: "generatedPaymentBatches" });
db.PaymentBatch.belongsTo(db.User, { foreignKey: "generated_by", as: "generator" });

db.PaymentBatch.hasMany(db.PaymentBatchItem, { foreignKey: "payment_batch_id", as: "items" });
db.PaymentBatchItem.belongsTo(db.PaymentBatch, { foreignKey: "payment_batch_id", as: "paymentBatch" });
db.PaymentBatchItem.belongsTo(db.PayrollLine, { foreignKey: "payroll_line_id", as: "payrollLine" });
db.PaymentBatchItem.belongsTo(db.Staff, { foreignKey: "staff_id", as: "staff" });

db.PaymentBatch.hasMany(db.Payslip, { foreignKey: "payment_batch_id", as: "payslips" });
db.Payslip.belongsTo(db.PaymentBatch, { foreignKey: "payment_batch_id", as: "paymentBatch" });
db.Payslip.hasOne(db.PaymentBatchItem, { foreignKey: "payroll_line_id", sourceKey: "payroll_line_id", as: "paymentItem" });
db.Payslip.belongsTo(db.PayrollLine, { foreignKey: "payroll_line_id", as: "payrollLine" });
db.Staff.hasMany(db.Payslip, { foreignKey: "staff_id", as: "payslips" });
db.Payslip.belongsTo(db.Staff, { foreignKey: "staff_id", as: "staff" });
```
Plus `User.belongsTo(Staff)`/`Staff.hasOne(User)`, and audit/invoice associations not directly relevant to payment.

## 7. Seeder execution config

`backend/package.json` scripts:
```json
"db:migrate": "sequelize-cli db:migrate",
"db:migrate:undo": "sequelize-cli db:migrate:undo",
"db:seed": "sequelize-cli db:seed:all",
"db:seed:payments:test": "sequelize-cli db:seed --seed 20260726000100-development-payment-test-data.js",
"db:seed:undo": "sequelize-cli db:seed:undo:all"
```
`backend/.sequelizerc`:
```js
const path = require("path");
module.exports = {
    config: path.resolve("src/config/sequelizeCli.js"),
    "models-path": path.resolve("src/models"),
    "migrations-path": path.resolve("src/db/migrations"),
    "seeders-path": path.resolve("src/db/seeders"),
};
```
So `db:seed` runs both seeders in order (users then payment test data). There's also a dedicated script to run only the payment test-data seeder.

## 8. `backend/tests/` directory and `payment.test.js`

`backend/tests/` listing: `approval.test.js`, `auth.test.js`, `authorization.test.js`, `payment.test.js` (321 lines, full content above), `payrollCalc.test.js`, `payslip.test.js`, `rosterSync.test.js`, `validation.test.js`.

`payment.test.js` builds **all of its own fixtures** via a `fixture()` helper that creates a `PayPeriod`, `Staff`, `PayrollLine`, and `Approval` per test with fresh deterministic UUIDs (counter-based, prefix `92000000-...`), and tears everything down in `afterAll` by deleting rows scoped to `testPeriodIds`/`testStaffIds`/`testBatchIds`. It does **not** reference or depend on the dev seeder's data (the `DEV-PAY-*` scenarios) at all — the only shared dependency is the pre-existing `manager@payroll.local` / `employee@payroll.local` users (created by the authentication-users seeder, used for login in `beforeAll`). So the payment test-data seeder is purely for manual/local dev exploration of the frontend UI, not test fixtures.

## 9. Model field lists

**`backend/src/models/PayrollLine.js`** (table `payroll_line`): `id` (UUID PK), `pay_period_id` (UUID), `staff_id` (UUID), `gross_pay` (DECIMAL 12,2), `incentive_pay` (DECIMAL 12,2), `cpf_amount` (DECIMAL 12,2), `sdl_amount` (DECIMAL 12,2), `net_pay` (DECIMAL 12,2), `status` (ENUM: "ok","incomplete"). Plus timestamps.

**`backend/src/models/Approval.js`** (table `approval`): `id` (UUID PK), `pay_period_id` (UUID), `decision` (ENUM: "approved","rejected"), `approved_by` (STRING 100), `comment` (TEXT, nullable), `decided_at` (DATE, default now). `createdAt` only (no `updatedAt`).

